<p>Choosing a relational database means choosing where transactions run, which failures they survive and who will keep that arrangement working. An engine name answers only part of that question. The same engine on a laptop, on one server and behind a managed failover service gives three different operating contracts.</p>
<p>We will choose a starting system for a small shop’s reservation service. A customer reserves the last blue mug; the service must reduce stock and record the reservation together, while other customers try to buy it. We will turn that promise into rejection tests, narrow the deployment choices, run a bounded PostgreSQL evaluation and write a decision that says exactly how far its evidence goes.</p>
<p>The common working route starts with <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/#start-with-a-database-you-can-inspect-and-reset">a disposable PostgreSQL database in Working with SQL</a>. You can learn and test there while a production choice is still open. The SQLite and CockroachDB articles linked here are conditional checks for candidates that reach your shortlist.</p>

