<section aria-labelledby="begin-with-the-application-s-promises">
 <h2 id="begin-with-the-application-s-promises">Begin with the application’s promises</h2>
 <p>The shop stores products, customers, reservations and the work needed to send confirmations. Its critical read is a customer’s newest reservations. Its critical change claims one unit and records who claimed it. The stock must never become negative; an accepted request must identify one reservation; repeating that request must not reserve another unit.</p>
 <p>Put the stock change and reservation insert in one database transaction. A confirmation job can join that transaction; the actual email cannot. This gives us a correctness test before we choose a product. <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a> develops the transaction protocol, and <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a> handles retries and external effects.</p>
 <p>The following brief is invented for this selection case. The architecture is a design commitment; the volume and latency targets are forecasts, not measurements of a running shop. Replacing guesses with observations is part of the evaluation.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="The shop’s selection brief"><table><thead><tr><th>Need and status</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Rejection test</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Correctness · hard</td><td>No negative stock, orphan reservations or duplicate request identities. Stock and reservation agree after every outcome.</td><td>Overlapping claims, duplicate requests and interrupted clients must preserve the invariants.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Deployment · committed design</td><td>Three application hosts in one European region share the same stock; one background worker also writes.</td><td>Reject an arrangement requiring those hosts to open one local WAL file directly.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Volume · forecast</td><td>10 million retained reservations, about 100 GB including initial indexes; double that footprint in a year.</td><td>Test the forecast data and a doubled copy. Account separately for log, free space and backups.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Load · forecast</td><td>200 reads and 20 reservation attempts/s normally; tenfold bursts for 60 seconds. Half the burst claims target one product.</td><td>Meet the deadline during the hot-product burst, while verifying successful and refused claims.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Latency · proposed gate</td><td>At least 99% of reservation responses within 300 ms from API entry, including pool waits and retries; fewer than 0.1% infrastructure errors.</td><td>Measure through the intended driver and network. A legitimate sold-out response is a business result, not an infrastructure error.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Recovery · hard</td><td>No confirmed reservation lost on a database host failure; service restored within 15 minutes. For accidental deletion, restore to a point no more than five minutes before the mistake within one hour.</td><td>Verify acknowledged identities after the host failure; time a separate restore and validate application state.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Location and operations · hard</td><td>Primary data, replicas and backups stay in approved European locations. Two engineers can allocate four routine operator hours/month.</td><td>Reject unsupported placement or an operating plan without named cover for incidents and recovery.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cost · planning limit</td><td>£1,000/month for recurring database service and routine operator effort at the planning rate, with one-off migration work recorded separately.</td><td>Price the accepted topology at normal and burst load. A cheaper failing topology does not qualify.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <p>“Highly available” would hide two different needs here. A host failure must preserve every acknowledged reservation, whereas accidental deletion may lose up to five minutes of changes. Record each failure, its acceptable data loss and its recovery time separately. The shop currently accepts a whole-region outage; continuing writes through that outage is a possible future requirement, not a reason to distribute today.</p>
 <p>Keep negotiable preferences beside the gates: familiar tooling, a preferred provider and convenient dashboards. They help rank candidates that pass. They cannot compensate for losing confirmed orders. If every candidate fails a gate, change the design, the candidate or the requirement openly.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="establish-whether-a-relational-system-fits">
 <h2 id="establish-whether-a-relational-system-fits">Establish whether a relational system fits</h2>
 <p>The shop’s facts have identities and relationships, and one action changes several of them. Constraints can reject an unknown product, duplicate request or negative quantity, and transactions can keep the claim and its reservation together. Queries will change as the shop asks which customers have pending reservations or which products repeatedly sell out. Those are strong reasons to begin with a relational system.</p>
 <p>A document store becomes relevant if independently fetched documents are the natural unit of change and its actual transaction and query contract meets the same gates. A key-value store may serve a simple lookup path; a column store may serve large aggregations; a search engine may serve relevance ranking. None removes the need to decide who owns the authoritative stock.</p>
 <p>Keep the reservation truth in one place for this first design. Product photographs belong in object storage, with references in the database. If a search index later earns its cost, derive it from committed product changes and define how far it may lag. Two stores introduce a second recovery path and a synchronisation problem: restoring the database does not automatically restore the matching search state. Add the store when a requirement or measurement justifies that work.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="choose-the-process-and-failure-boundary">
 <h2 id="choose-the-process-and-failure-boundary">Choose the process and failure boundary</h2>
 <p>An <strong>embedded engine</strong> runs as a library inside the application. A <strong>database server</strong> owns the files and accepts requests from applications over connections. A <strong>distributed relational system</strong> coordinates data and transactions across machines. These boundaries change the network path, the way writers meet and what remains after a machine fails.</p>
 <p>Step through the embedded reservation, then select Server at the same step. Watch where a network wait and a second durable copy can enter. The last arrangement shows why a nearby SQL endpoint alone does not promise a nearby transaction.</p>
 <BoundaryFigure />
 <p>The engine, topology and hosting are separate decisions. PostgreSQL is an engine; a primary with a synchronous standby in another zone is a topology; self-management or a particular managed service is a hosting arrangement. Record all three. “SQL compatible” establishes neither equal isolation nor equal supported features.</p>

 <h3 id="when-the-database-lives-with-the-application">When the database lives with the application</h3>
 <p>SQLite is attractive when one application or one host owns its data: an offline device, a desktop application or a service whose local writers fit the file’s concurrency contract. It removes the server process and database network hop. The application team still owns file permissions, persistent storage, backups and recovery after losing the host.</p>
 <p>SQLite permits one writer at a time. That is a question about the duration and overlap of writes, not a universal row-count or request-rate limit. Its <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html">documented WAL arrangement requires connections on the same host</a>. The shop’s proposed three hosts directly sharing that WAL file therefore fail the deployment gate. Putting SQLite behind a single-host API changes the candidate: it introduces a service boundary and leaves host survival to solve.</p>
 <p>If a local file fits your actual application, take <a href="/review/relational-next/sqlite/">Checking an embedded choice with SQLite</a>. It verifies the loaded library, driver, busy handling, durability settings and recoverable files. This shop can reject its direct shared-file arrangement without completing that optional route.</p>

 <h3 id="when-applications-share-a-database-service">When applications share a database service</h3>
 <p>A server gives all three application hosts one place to change stock. PostgreSQL is our first candidate because the common exercises already establish a schema and SQL baseline, and the team knows its tooling. An existing MySQL estate, a required extension or a support agreement could produce a different shortlist. Those are reasons tied to this application and team; they do not rank engines for every workload.</p>
 <p>Read replicas can take suitable reads away from a primary, but an asynchronous replica may return an older reservation list. Keep the shop’s “show what I just reserved” read on the primary until a specific freshness protocol is tested. Adding replicas also does not move ordinary primary writes off that primary.</p>
 <p>The bounded candidate actually exercised here is <strong>PostgreSQL 18.6, self-managed native server, one primary on a local Apple-silicon host; no managed service tier</strong>. The path is psql 18.6 through libpq and IPv4 loopback to that server, with no TLS, ORM or pooler. It is a disposable verification deployment, not the intended three-host production path.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="PostgreSQL starting contract and its limits"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Verified starting value or decision</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Connections</td><td>12 total; 3 reserved for superusers; 0 additional reserved connections.</td><td>Ordinary application roles cannot treat all 12 as their budget. Four test clients fit; production pools must share a measured budget with maintenance and other services.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Session and pool features</td><td>Direct sessions; no pooler tested. No application extension required or tested.</td><td>Prepared statements, session settings, temporary objects and authentication must be checked if the actual driver or pool mode uses them.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Transactions</td><td>Read committed; guarded stock UPDATE plus reservation INSERT in one statement.</td><td>The atomic statement is the tested claim protocol. The isolation label alone does not make any read-then-write application correct.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Durability</td><td>fsync, full_page_writes and synchronous_commit on; synchronous_standby_names empty.</td><td>The last value means no remote standby acknowledgement is awaited. This topology cannot prove the host-loss requirement.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Timeouts</td><td>Statement 30 s; lock wait 5 s; idle transaction 60 s.</td><td>These bound the lab, but exceed the proposed 300 ms API budget. Production deadlines and cancellation need their own application-path test.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Privileges and transport</td><td>Local trusted test role, loopback only. Production role separation and certificate-verified TLS untested.</td><td>A working privileged local harness does not establish that the application role can operate safely in the deployed network.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <p>These settings were read back from the server on 19 October 2026. The PostgreSQL 18 manuals define <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-connection.html">connection reservations</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-wal.html">the durability settings</a>. Keep this candidate record beside the common articles’ <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/">SQL results</a>, <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">concurrency cases</a>, <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">workload method</a> and <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">recovery gates</a>. Their observations transfer only where version, settings and execution path match.</p>

 <h3 id="when-data-and-writes-must-span-machines-or-regions">When data and writes must span machines or regions</h3>
 <p>Suppose the shop later requires correct reservations while an entire region is unavailable, or measured capacity no longer fits one primary after reasonable improvements. Now compare a distributed candidate against a server topology that could meet the same requirement. Merely having customers in several countries does not establish either need.</p>
 <p>CockroachDB’s <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/multiregion-overview">version 26.3 multi-region documentation</a> distinguishes table locality and survival goals. Those controls change replica placement and coordination. A cross-region transaction or a hot stock row can still dominate latency; selecting a region in a console does not establish data residency, survival or cost.</p>
 <p>Enter <a href="/review/relational-next/cockroachdb/">Checking a distributed choice with CockroachDB</a> only when that requirement survives comparison. It pins the deployment and commercial conditions, then checks SQL compatibility, placement, retries, measured latency and failures. Nothing in the local PostgreSQL run here establishes acceptance for CockroachDB.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="compare-the-deployment-contract-and-its-full-cost">
 <h2 id="compare-the-deployment-contract-and-its-full-cost">Compare the deployment contract and its full cost</h2>
 <p>A managed service can run the database software while leaving the application team responsible for whether its backups are usable and its failover fits the request path. Assign each duty to a person or team, and retain the dated service documentation or contract that makes the assignment credible.</p>
 <p>Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL is a concrete managed-service comparison here, not a provisioned or accepted candidate. Its <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Concepts.MultiAZSingleStandby.html">Multi-AZ DB instance arrangement</a> maintains a synchronous standby in another availability zone; that standby does not serve read traffic. A Multi-AZ DB cluster is a different arrangement. The label “Multi-AZ” needs the deployment type beside it.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Who must prove the deployment contract"><table><thead><tr><th>Obligation</th><th>Self-managed PostgreSQL</th><th>RDS comparison and remaining owner</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Version, features and access</td><td>Database operator installs the pinned release, extensions and role policy.</td><td>Provider supplies supported builds and extensions. The team checks exact engine version, class, region and parameter group before evaluation; none has been selected here. RDS gives rds_superuser rather than PostgreSQL superuser or host OS access. Application lead verifies required SQL and privileges.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Capacity and connections</td><td>Operator sizes resources and reserves maintenance headroom; application lead bounds all pools.</td><td>Operator checks chosen class, storage/I/O, autoscaling and connection limits; application lead tests its driver and pool mode. No RDS capacity limit has been measured here.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Maintenance and upgrades</td><td>Operator owns patching, compatibility rehearsal and recovery plan.</td><td>Provider runs the supported upgrade workflow. Operator selects permitted timing and rehearses application reconnection and compatibility. RDS documents possible unavailability during Multi-AZ DB instance engine upgrades.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Location and protection</td><td>Operator places all copies, restricts network access and protects keys; security owner reviews access and audit needs.</td><td>Operator verifies region, replicas, snapshots, export location, private connectivity, encryption and key recovery for the selected configuration. Security owner approves and tests the access boundary.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Failover and restoration</td><td>Operator builds and tests replicas, promotion, backups, retained logs and restore capacity.</td><td>Provider supplies the configured facilities. Operator checks retention, restore granularity and failure contract; application lead checks reconnects and uncertain requests. Operator proves timed restore and application validation.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <p>The RDS restrictions above come from its <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Appendix.PostgreSQL.CommonDBATasks.Roles.rds_superuser.html">role documentation</a> and <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_UpgradeDBInstance.PostgreSQL.html">upgrade documentation</a>, checked on 19 October 2026. Verify the selected configuration again before adoption. A service availability commitment is contractual evidence; it is not an observed restore time for your data.</p>
 <p>Price the topology that passes, including compute and memory at idle and peak, storage growth, indexes and log, I/O charges, standby and read-replica capacity, backups beyond allowances, restore compute, network transfer, support and licences. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/about/licence/">permissive licence</a> does not make its hosting or engineering free. Check any extension’s and alternative product’s own terms separately.</p>
 <p>In the model below, the self-managed infrastructure allowance is lower. Move its monthly operator time down and watch when that becomes a lower total. The managed option still needs four hours because the team retains application, access and recovery work.</p>
 <CostFigure />
 <p>At twelve self-managed hours, the £1,220 allowance exceeds the shop’s £1,000 planning limit; the £780 managed allowance leaves £220. Those are arithmetic results from invented inputs. They support asking for a managed-service quote, not accepting its price. An outage’s response cover and occasional recovery work may exceed the routine-hour allowance, so record who provides that cover and how it is paid.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="test-the-candidates-against-the-same-application">
 <h2 id="test-the-candidates-against-the-same-application">Test the candidates against the same application</h2>
 <p>First screen the arrangements against the promises. Change the requirement below from our shared service to an offline device, then to whole-region survival. Watch which specific boundary fails; an engine is not rejected for every topology merely because one arrangement fails.</p>
 <GateFigure />
 <p>The shop shortlists a PostgreSQL service, rejects direct multi-host access to one SQLite WAL file and postpones distributed SQL. Its next question is whether the proposed PostgreSQL contract actually preserves a reservation.</p>
 <p>Our bounded run used inventory initially containing seven P7 mugs and a reservation table with a unique request identity, a non-null positive exact numeric amount and a foreign key to inventory. A conditional UPDATE claims a unit only while one remains; the INSERT takes its input from the claimed row:</p>
 <Sql code={reservation} label="PostgreSQL 18: claim stock and record one reservation in the same statement" />
 <p>One returned identity means a reservation was recorded. No returned row means this statement found no claimable unit. A duplicate identity raises a uniqueness error; the statement’s stock decrement rolls back with it. The application must then look up the existing request and compare its original arguments before treating it as the same operation. It must also reconcile a lost commit reply. This small statement is a candidate check, not the complete request protocol.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Observed PostgreSQL selection evidence"><table><thead><tr><th>Case, observed 19 October 2026</th><th>Result</th><th>What it establishes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Attempt negative stock, unknown product and null amount</td><td>Rejected with SQLSTATE 23514, 23503 and 23502 respectively.</td><td>The exercised check, foreign key and non-null constraint operate on this schema and server.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Accept the first request, then repeat its identity while stock remains</td><td>Repeat rejected with 23505; stock stayed at six.</td><td>A uniqueness failure in this statement did not leave its earlier decrement applied.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Four persistent psql sessions issue 25 distinct attempts each against those six remaining units</td><td>Final stock zero; seven total reservations including the initial one; amount total 140.00.</td><td>The tested concurrent workload did not oversell. This is not a throughput or tail-latency result.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Logical dump and re-import into a fresh database on the same server</td><td>Stock, count, total and every ordered reservation row matched.</td><td>The small schema and data can be exported and re-imported with PostgreSQL tools. This does not prove host-loss recovery or point-in-time recovery.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <p>Only those cases are observed. The run had four client sessions, one inventory row and seven successful reservations, using the bounded server settings above. It did not contain ten million reservations, a 60-second production burst, slow clients, the application driver, a pooler, TLS or a remote network. No server, disk, zone or region was failed. Do not put a green tick beside the production gates on the strength of this table.</p>
 <p>Continue with the actual application image and driver version, application role, pool configuration and deployed network. Restore representative data with realistic customer skew, row widths and indexes; run ordinary reads alongside the hot-product burst. Verify returned identities, refusal reasons and final invariants before comparing completed throughput, p50/p95/p99 response times, resource use and cost. Include connection pressure and slow clients; count pool waits, retries and refused infrastructure operations rather than timing only successful SQL.</p>
 <p>For the host-loss gate, use an authorised disposable deployment matching the proposed topology. Record confirmed request identities outside it, fail the named host, reconnect through the intended endpoint and compare those identities with surviving reservations. Measure interruption and latency while capacity returns. Separately restore from protected backup and retained log to the required point, then validate the application. <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">The performance article</a> supplies the workload procedure; <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a> supplies the operating and recovery route.</p>
 <p>Keep three evidence types separate in the result: <strong>documented</strong> product behaviour; <strong>observed</strong> results with version, settings, workload and path; and <strong>untested</strong> acceptance gaps. An animation or a vendor benchmark can explain a hypothesis without testing this shop’s deployment.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="make-the-choice-reversible-enough-to-live-with">
 <h2 id="make-the-choice-reversible-enough-to-live-with">Make the choice reversible enough to live with</h2>
 <p>A decision record should let the next engineer see why the choice was reasonable and what would make it stop being reasonable. Include the team’s ability to operate it, the supported driver and tooling, incident cover and an exit route alongside the database results.</p>
 <p>Here is the shop’s decision on the evidence we actually have:</p>
 <div class="decision"><p><strong>Decision · 19 October 2026.</strong> Adopt PostgreSQL 18.6 on the recorded local single-primary configuration for the disposable application prototype. The observed data-contract and small logical-export gates pass. Reject that single-host topology for production: it has no independently placed surviving copy to satisfy the host-loss gate.</p>
 <p><strong>Production adoption is deferred.</strong> Evaluate a managed PostgreSQL service first because the operating-hour allowance favours it. Before treating RDS as a candidate, the database operator must name and record the exact available engine minor version, region, instance class, storage configuration, parameter group, Multi-AZ deployment type, retention and quoted price. No provisioned RDS tier, failover, restore or price acceptance is claimed here.</p>
 <p><strong>Alternative decisions.</strong> Reject directly shared multi-host SQLite WAL for this deployment. Keep SQLite available for an offline or single-host product. Defer CockroachDB until a measured capacity or failure requirement justifies its separate evaluation. MySQL remains a possible server alternative if existing team support or a required integration makes it cheaper to prove the same contract.</p></div>
 <p>Complete the remaining record with owners and stop criteria:</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Outstanding adoption gates and owners"><table><thead><tr><th>Owner</th><th>Evidence still required</th><th>Stop or reopen condition</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Application lead</td><td>Pinned driver/ORM/pool path; request replay and uncertain-outcome checks; correct results and 300 ms p99 under forecast burst.</td><td>Stop rollout for invariant failure, duplicate effect or deadline/error-rate breach.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Database operator</td><td>Production topology and connection budget; host failover with zero acknowledged loss and ≤15-minute recovery; protected point-in-time restore with ≤5-minute loss and ≤1-hour recovery.</td><td>No critical data until these pass. Reopen on topology, volume, retention or failure-goal change.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Security owner</td><td>Least-privilege roles, verified TLS, approved locations for all copies, audit and recoverable encryption keys.</td><td>Stop if required access or placement cannot be established.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Service owner</td><td>Dated recurring quote plus measured normal/burst resource use, support and operator effort; named incident cover.</td><td>Stop for a forecast above £1,000/month or unsupported operating responsibility.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <p>The local connection ceiling is configuration evidence, not measured capacity. The seven-reservation run establishes no candidate throughput limit. Keep those fields explicitly unmeasured until the production-shaped evaluation supplies them.</p>
 <p>For exit planning, inventory PostgreSQL-specific SQL, exact numeric and timestamp representations, identity generation, collations, extensions and operational dependencies. Our same-engine dump/re-import checks one useful route; it proves no cross-engine compatibility. If the exit requirement is another engine, import a representative export there and compare constraints, values and critical query results. Include export access, credentials, keys and the time needed to transfer data.</p>
 <p>Stage adoption through a disposable prototype, a representative deployment and a limited production rollout after the gates pass. Recheck correctness and recovery evidence after a material change. A future database move needs coexistence, cutover and rollback work; <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/#switch-traffic-only-when-the-destination-is-ready">Changing a live database</a> owns that procedure.</p>
 <p>If the shortlisted boundary is an embedded file, continue with <a href="/review/relational-next/sqlite/">Checking an embedded choice with SQLite</a>. If a demonstrated requirement calls for distributed relational transactions, continue with <a href="/review/relational-next/cockroachdb/">Checking a distributed choice with CockroachDB</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="essay-sources" aria-labelledby="sources">
 <h2 id="sources">Sources and further reading</h2>
 <p>Primary documentation checked on 19 October 2026. Native observations use PostgreSQL 18.6 (Homebrew), psql/libpq, a local single-primary server and the recorded bounded settings. The shop, requirements and cost allowances are illustrative. RDS, SQLite and CockroachDB deployments were not run for this article.</p>
